{
  "id": "proofs.divisibility.divides-product-converse",
  "course": "proofs",
  "topic": "divisibility",
  "kind": "counterexample",
  "title": "Disprove: a | bc means a | b or a | c",
  "prompt": "Disprove: for all integers $a$, $b$, $c$, if $a \\mid bc$ then $a \\mid b$ or $a \\mid c$. Give a counterexample.",
  "answer_format": "Integer values for every variable, like a = 4, b = 2, c = 2.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "counterexample",
    "divisibility"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": {
      "a": "integer",
      "b": "integer",
      "c": "integer"
    },
    "hypothesis": {
      "op": "divides",
      "args": [
        "a",
        "b c"
      ]
    },
    "conclusion": {
      "op": "or",
      "args": [
        {
          "op": "divides",
          "args": [
            "a",
            "b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "divides",
          "args": [
            "a",
            "c"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "answer": "a = 4, b = 2, c = 2",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "You need a to divide the product bc, but divide neither b nor c on its own.",
    "Try a composite a, and split its factors between b and c."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Take $a = 4$, $b = 2$, $c = 2$.",
    "Then $bc = 4$, so $a \\mid bc$. But $4 \\nmid 2$, so $a \\nmid b$ and $a \\nmid c$.",
    "The hypothesis holds and the conclusion fails, so the statement is false. (It *is* true when $a$ is prime.)"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "hammack-bop3",
    "section": "9.1 Counterexamples",
    "note": "Original problem; see Chapter 9, Disproof."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "retired",
    "notes": "Invented problem, retired 2026-11-06: MATH 123 proofs now map 1:1 to Book of Proof exercises."
  },
